# 🚀 Complete Guide: Running OCI ARM Capacity Script on Your Free Instance

You have a **VM.Standard.E2.1.Micro** (1/8 OCPU, 1 GB RAM) instance and want to run the `oci-arm-host-capacity` PHP script continuously to snag an ARM instance when capacity opens up.

---

## 📋 What You Need (Checklist)

| # | Item | Where to Get It |
|---|------|-----------------|
| 1 | **OCI API Key** (private `.pem` file + fingerprint) | OCI Console → Profile → User Settings → API Keys |
| 2 | **OCI Region** | From your tenancy (e.g. `ap-mumbai-1`, `us-ashburn-1`) |
| 3 | **User OCID** | OCI Console → Profile → User Settings |
| 4 | **Tenancy OCID** | OCI Console → Profile → Tenancy |
| 5 | **Subnet ID** | Captured from browser dev tools (see Step 2 below) |
| 6 | **Image ID** | Captured from browser dev tools (see Step 2 below) |
| 7 | **SSH Public Key** | Your `~/.ssh/id_rsa.pub` (or generate one) |
| 8 | **PHP 7.x or 8.x** | Install on instance |
| 9 | **Composer** | Install on instance |
| 10 | **git** | Install on instance |

---

## Step 1: Generate OCI API Key (in OCI Console)

1. Log in to [OCI Console](https://cloud.oracle.com/)
2. Click your **profile icon** (top-right) → **User Settings**
3. Go to **Resources** → **API Keys** → **Add API Key**
4. Select **"Generate API Key Pair"**
5. Click **"Download Private Key"** → save the `.pem` file (you'll upload this to your instance later)
6. Click **"Add"**
7. **Copy the configuration preview** that appears — it contains:
   ```
   [DEFAULT]
   user=ocid1.user.oc1..aaaa...
   fingerprint=xx:xx:xx:...
   tenancy=ocid1.tenancy.oc1..aaaa...
   region=your-region
   key_file=<path to your private key>
   ```
   
> [!IMPORTANT]
> Save this information — you'll need the **user OCID**, **tenancy OCID**, **fingerprint**, and **region** for the `.env` file.

---

## Step 2: Get Subnet ID & Image ID (from Browser Dev Tools)

This is the trickiest part. You need to **pretend** to create an ARM instance to capture the API request parameters.

1. In OCI Console → **Menu** → **Compute** → **Instances** → **Create Instance**
2. Change the **Image**: Pick your desired OS (e.g., Ubuntu 22.04, Oracle Linux 8)
3. Change the **Shape**: Select **Ampere** → **VM.Standard.A1.Flex** → set 4 OCPUs / 24 GB RAM
4. In **Networking**: use your existing VCN/Subnet, **uncheck** "Assign a public IPv4 address"
5. **Before clicking "Create"** → Open browser **DevTools** (F12) → go to the **Network** tab
6. Click **"Create"** — you'll likely get an "Out of capacity" error
7. In DevTools Network tab, find the **red** `/instances` API call
8. **Right-click** → **Copy as cURL**
9. Paste in a text editor and find:
   - `"subnetId": "ocid1.subnet.oc1..."` → this is your **`OCI_SUBNET_ID`**
   - `"imageId": "ocid1.image.oc1..."` → this is your **`OCI_IMAGE_ID`**
   - Note the `"availabilityDomain"` value too (e.g., `jYtI:AP-MUMBAI-1-AD-1`)

> [!TIP]
> For ARM instances, you can leave `OCI_AVAILABILITY_DOMAIN` empty — the script will auto-discover all ADs and try each one.

---

## Step 3: Set Up Your Instance (SSH Commands)

You've SSHed into your OCI instance via VS Code. Now run these commands:

### 3a. Update system & install PHP + Composer + git

**For Oracle Linux / CentOS (most likely on OCI):**
```bash
# Update packages
sudo yum update -y

# Enable PHP module (Oracle Linux 8+)
sudo dnf module enable php:8.0 -y  # or php:8.1

# Install PHP and required extensions
sudo yum install -y php php-cli php-curl php-json php-mbstring php-xml git unzip

# Verify PHP
php -v

# Install Composer
curl -sS https://getcomposer.org/installer | php
sudo mv composer.phar /usr/local/bin/composer

# Verify Composer
composer --version
```

**For Ubuntu (if your instance runs Ubuntu):**
```bash
# Update packages
sudo apt update && sudo apt upgrade -y

# Install PHP and required extensions
sudo apt install -y php php-cli php-curl php-mbstring php-xml git unzip

# Verify PHP
php -v

# Install Composer
curl -sS https://getcomposer.org/installer | php
sudo mv composer.phar /usr/local/bin/composer

# Verify Composer
composer --version
```

### 3b. Clone the repo & install dependencies

```bash
cd ~
git clone https://github.com/hitrov/oci-arm-host-capacity.git
cd oci-arm-host-capacity
composer install
```

---

## Step 4: Upload Your Private Key

Upload the `.pem` file you downloaded in Step 1 to your instance. From your **local machine** (PowerShell):

```powershell
# Replace with your actual values
scp -i C:\Users\pmadh\.ssh\id_rsa C:\path\to\your-private-key.pem opc@<YOUR_INSTANCE_IP>:~/.oci/
```

Or if you're using VS Code Remote SSH, you can just **drag and drop** the `.pem` file into the file explorer.

On the instance, create the directory and set permissions:
```bash
mkdir -p ~/.oci
# Move the .pem file here if not already
chmod 600 ~/.oci/your-private-key.pem
```

---

## Step 5: Generate SSH Key Pair (if you don't have one on the instance)

This SSH key will be injected into the **new ARM instance** you're trying to create:

```bash
# Generate a new SSH key pair (on the OCI instance)
ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -C "your@email.com"
# Press Enter for default path, set passphrase or leave empty

# View the public key (you'll need this for .env)
cat ~/.ssh/id_rsa.pub
# or
cat ~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub
```

Copy the **entire output** — you'll paste it into `OCI_SSH_PUBLIC_KEY`.

---

## Step 6: Configure the `.env` File

```bash
cd ~/oci-arm-host-capacity
cp .env.example .env
nano .env
```

Fill in **all values** like this:

```env
OCI_REGION=ap-mumbai-1
OCI_USER_ID=ocid1.user.oc1..aaaaaaa_YOUR_USER_OCID
OCI_TENANCY_ID=ocid1.tenancy.oc1..aaaaaaa_YOUR_TENANCY_OCID
OCI_KEY_FINGERPRINT=xx:xx:xx:xx:xx:xx:xx:xx:xx:xx:xx:xx:xx:xx:xx:xx

OCI_PRIVATE_KEY_FILENAME="/home/opc/.oci/your-private-key.pem"
OCI_SUBNET_ID=ocid1.subnet.oc1.ap-mumbai-1.aaaaaaa_FROM_STEP_2
OCI_IMAGE_ID=ocid1.image.oc1.ap-mumbai-1.aaaaaaa_FROM_STEP_2

# For the full free ARM instance (4 OCPUs, 24 GB RAM)
OCI_OCPUS=4
OCI_MEMORY_IN_GBS=24

OCI_SHAPE=VM.Standard.A1.Flex
OCI_MAX_INSTANCES=1

OCI_SSH_PUBLIC_KEY="ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1N... your@email.com"

# Leave empty for ARM — script auto-discovers all ADs
OCI_AVAILABILITY_DOMAIN=

CACHE_AVAILABILITY_DOMAINS=1
OCI_BOOT_VOLUME_SIZE_IN_GBS=
OCI_BOOT_VOLUME_ID=
TOO_MANY_REQUESTS_TIME_WAIT=600
```

> [!WARNING]
> - Make sure `OCI_SSH_PUBLIC_KEY` is on **ONE line** — no line breaks!
> - Make sure the `.pem` file path is **absolute** (starts with `/`)
> - Use your **actual** region, not `eu-frankfurt-1` from the example

Save: `Ctrl+O` → `Enter` → `Ctrl+X`

---

## Step 7: Test the Script

```bash
cd ~/oci-arm-host-capacity
php index.php
```

Expected output (this is normal — it means the script is working correctly):
```json
{
    "code": "InternalError",
    "message": "Out of host capacity."
}
```

If you get a different error, see [Troubleshooting](#troubleshooting) below.

---

## Step 8: Set Up Cron Job (Run Continuously Every Minute)

```bash
# Create log file
touch ~/oci-arm-host-capacity/oci.log
chmod 777 ~/oci-arm-host-capacity/oci.log

# Find PHP path
which php
# Usually: /usr/bin/php

# Set up cron job
crontab -e
```

If prompted, choose `nano` as editor. Add this line at the bottom:

```
* * * * * /usr/bin/php /home/opc/oci-arm-host-capacity/index.php >> /home/opc/oci-arm-host-capacity/oci.log 2>&1
```

> [!NOTE]
> - Replace `/home/opc` with your actual home directory (run `echo $HOME` to check)
> - Replace `/usr/bin/php` with the output of `which php`
> - This runs the script **every minute, 24/7**

Save: `Ctrl+O` → `Enter` → `Ctrl+X`

Verify cron is set:
```bash
crontab -l
```

---

## Step 9: Monitor the Log

```bash
# Watch the log in real-time
tail -f ~/oci-arm-host-capacity/oci.log
```

When the script successfully creates an instance, you'll see a large JSON output with instance details instead of the "Out of host capacity" error.

---

## 🔧 Troubleshooting

| Error | Fix |
|-------|-----|
| `Private key file does not exist` | Check `OCI_PRIVATE_KEY_FILENAME` path is absolute and file exists |
| `Permission denied` on `.pem` file | Run `chmod 600 ~/.oci/your-key.pem` |
| `Unable to parse message body` | Your `OCI_SSH_PUBLIC_KEY` has line breaks — put on single line |
| `Invalid ssh public key` | Copy the key from `~/.ssh/id_rsa.pub` again, ensure no extra characters |
| `NotAuthenticated` | Check User OCID, Tenancy OCID, Fingerprint, and Region match your API key |
| `TooManyRequests (429)` | Normal — the script auto-waits 600 seconds (configurable via `TOO_MANY_REQUESTS_TIME_WAIT`) |

---

## ⚡ Pro Tips

1. **Upgrade to PAYG** (Pay As You Go) — free benefits remain the same, but you get **priority** for instance launches and fewer "Out of Capacity" errors. Set up budget alerts as a safety net.

2. **Telegram notifications** — Set `TELEGRAM_BOT_API_KEY` and `TELEGRAM_USER_ID` in `.env` to get a Telegram message when the instance is created. This way you don't have to monitor the log.

3. **After success** — Remember to:
   - Remove the cron job: `crontab -e` and delete the line
   - Assign a public IP to the new ARM instance (OCI Console → Instance → Attached VNICs → IPv4 Addresses → Edit → Ephemeral)

4. **Memory considerations** — Your 1 GB instance is more than enough for this PHP script. It's very lightweight.

5. **Multiple ADs** — Leave `OCI_AVAILABILITY_DOMAIN` empty so the script tries **all** availability domains in your region automatically.
